From c061ea215e48897e40a135e6d5b0dd5f7ceb62e8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 09:01:42 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] xwayland/present: a copied present keeps both eyes of a
 stereo window

A window that holds two eyes (version 4 of the KDE stereo declaration)
presents a pixmap twice its width. It can be flipped only where any pixmap
can: not when a child window clips the window, the present has an offset or
an update region, the pixmap has another size than the window or its buffer
cannot go to the compositor as it is. Such a present was copied into the
window, one eye wide, which keeps the left eye; the compositor, told that
the window holds two eyes, showed half of the left view to each.

A copy now keeps both. The window's pixmap becomes twice the window wide
(the picture it had goes to both halves), present_execute_copy() copies the
left eye as for any window, and the right eye goes to the same place in the
right half, clipped as the left eye is. What other windows of the surface
draw (a child over the stereo window) is 2D and drawn into the left half
only: before the pixmap goes to the compositor, and at every such copy, the
damage outside the stereo window's own area is copied to the right half.
Damage is posted for both halves, and the buffers the window's pixmap is
swapped with are kept in step for both.

The present completes as a flip: for a window that holds two eyes the
completion mode says whether both eyes reached the window's surface, so a
client that reads PresentCompleteNotify knows that a copy lost one. Where the
pair cannot be kept (no surface, a window smaller than its surface, a bordered
surface window, no memory) the copy and its completion are as before.

A window that no longer declares two eyes, or gets a present without them,
has its pixmap made one window wide again at once, with the left eye, so the
compositor never has a buffer of two eyes for a window that declares none.

The stereo pair test of the xwayland suite gains a window covered by a child
window of its own, a present with an offset and a window that stops
declaring two eyes. No compositor maps a surface in that test, so the client
reads the pixmap the server keeps for the window, as a compositor's window
manager can (CompositeNameWindowPixmap): twice the window wide with the left
eye red and the right eye blue at the same place, the child's own picture in
both, and one window wide again after the declaration is deleted.

Assisted-by: Claude Fable 5.2
